const title = "Sub-Zero Refrigerator Problems by Series — Same Day";
const description = "Sub-Zero 500/600 legacy, BI built-in, and 700/undercounter units fail in different ways. A series guide to common problems, codes, and parts availability.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Sub-Zero fridge warm but the freezer still cold?",
    a: "On a dual-refrigeration Sub-Zero — the 500, 600, and BI built-in series all use two separate cooling circuits — a warm fresh-food side with a cold freezer is most commonly the fresh-food evaporator fan motor, or a dirty condenser choking the sealed system. Because the two circuits are independent, one side can fail while the other keeps working normally. Our techs test the fan and check the condenser before quoting. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What does 'Vacuum Condenser' or EC 50 mean on a Sub-Zero?",
    a: "Both are efficiency warnings on the electronic-control units (600 series and the BI built-in), not the older analog 500 series. 'Vacuum Condenser' flashing means the unit is running inefficiently or the temperature is too high — most often a dust-clogged condenser, a worn door seal, or a cooling fault. EC 50 (with 'Service' flashing) means the refrigerator compressor has been running excessively. The first step is cleaning the condenser; if the temperature stays high after that, don't keep clearing it — it needs a diagnostic."
  },
  {
    q: "Why is my Sub-Zero wine cooler or undercounter unit not cooling?",
    a: "On undercounter and wine units like the UC-24 and 424, the leading cause is a dust-clogged condenser in the tight cabinet cavity, followed by a failed condenser fan motor. On a dual-zone wine unit, one zone reaching temperature while the other stays warm usually points to icing on that zone's evaporator or its fan not running. The sealed system itself is rarely the first suspect — and on the 424, a sealed-system leak is often still inside Sub-Zero's 12-year sealed-system coverage. See our wine cooler repair page for detail."
  },
  {
    q: "Can you still get parts for an old Sub-Zero?",
    a: "In almost every case, yes. Sub-Zero states it attempts to keep replacement parts available for about 20 years, and in practice parts are still sourced for units built in the 1980s. When an original part number is retired it is usually superseded by a current one rather than dropped — one current built-in control board, for example, replaces nine earlier board numbers, and one current 700/undercounter condenser-fan motor replaces four. Genuine dead-ends are rare and mostly affect pre-1995 legacy models."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing an old Sub-Zero?",
    a: "Usually yes — Sub-Zero built-ins are engineered for a design life of at least 20 years, and a typical major repair runs far less than replacing a $10,000–$15,000+ unit. The full repair-versus-replace math, with the numbers, is in our Sub-Zero lifespan guide. The short version: for a single-component failure on a unit under about 22 to 25 years old, repair is almost always the lower per-year cost."
  }
];

